## Review — the leak and the illegal read

Edit `review1/solution.tsx`. `ViewportBadge({ createObserver, onDisconnect })`
renders a `<section data-testid="frame">` and a `role="status"` paragraph
reading `Height: <n>`. It is written, it compiles, and it is wrong twice:

```tsx
const heightRef = useRef(0);

useEffect(() => {
  const observer = createObserver((height) => {
    heightRef.current = height;
  });
  observer.observe(frameRef.current!);
});

return (
  <>
    <section data-testid="frame" ref={frameRef} />
    <p role="status">Height: {heightRef.current}</p>
  </>
);
```

Fix both faults without changing what is on screen:

1. the height is read during render from a ref, so the paragraph is stale — the
   gate reports `240`, re-renders for an unrelated reason, and requires the
   paragraph to read `Height: 240` at the first commit after the report, not
   later;
2. the observer is never disconnected and is recreated on every render — the
   gate re-renders three times and requires `createObserver` to have been called
   once, then unmounts and requires exactly one `onDisconnect`.

```
uv run dojo test react v3ra03_refs_and_measurement review1
```

references.solution.tsx:
// v3ra03_refs_and_measurement / review1 - reference solution.
import { useCallback, useState } from "react";

type Observer = {
  observe: (node: Element) => void;
  disconnect: () => void;
};

export function ViewportBadge({
  createObserver,
  onDisconnect,
}: {
  createObserver: (onHeight: (height: number) => void) => Observer;
  onDisconnect: () => void;
}) {
  // Fault 1: the paragraph displays this, so it is state. A ref schedules
  // nothing, so the number on screen would lag until an unrelated re-render.
  const [height, setHeight] = useState(0);

  // Fault 2: one observer, created when the node attaches and retired when it
  // detaches. The old effect had no dependency array (a new observer per
  // commit) and no cleanup (nothing ever disconnected).
  const attach = useCallback(
    (node: HTMLElement) => {
      const observer = createObserver(setHeight);
      observer.observe(node);
      return () => {
        observer.disconnect();
        onDisconnect();
      };
    },
    [createObserver, onDisconnect],
  );

  return (
    <>
      <section data-testid="frame" ref={attach} />
      <p role="status">Height: {height}</p>
    </>
  );
}

lessons.fullReadme:
# V3·RA4 — Reducers and state modelling (archive)

## When to reach for a reducer

Not "when the state is complex" — that is too vague to act on. Three concrete
signals:

1. **Several values change together.** Two setters that must always be called as
   a pair are one piece of state that has been split.
2. **The next value depends on more than the previous value of one field.**
   "Adding an item also clears the coupon and recomputes eligibility" is a
   transition, not an assignment.
3. **Some actions are invalid in some states.** Once you write your second
   `if (status === "loading") return;` at a call site, the rules want to live in
   one place.

If none of those apply, `useState` is the right tool and a reducer is ceremony.

## Worked example — the shape of a good reducer

```tsx
type Item = { id: string; name: string; quantity: number };
type State = { items: Item[]; coupon: string | null };

export type CartAction =
  | { type: "added"; item: Item }
  | { type: "removed"; id: string }
  | { type: "quantityChanged"; id: string; quantity: number }
  | { type: "couponApplied"; code: string }
  | { type: "cleared" };

export function cartReducer(state: State, action: CartAction): State {
  switch (action.type) {
    case "added":
      return { ...state, items: [...state.items, action.item] };
    case "removed": {
      const items = state.items.filter((item) => item.id !== action.id);
      // Nothing matched: hand back the same object so React bails out.
      return items.length === state.items.length ? state : { ...state, items };
    }
    case "quantityChanged":
      return {
        ...state,
        items: state.items.map((item) =>
          item.id === action.id ? { ...item, quantity: action.quantity } : item,
        ),
      };
    case "couponApplied":
      return { ...state, coupon: action.code };
    case "cleared":
      return state.items.length === 0 ? state : { items: [], coupon: null };
    default: {
      const impossible: never = action;
      throw new Error(`Unhandled action: ${JSON.stringify(impossible)}`);
    }
  }
}
```

Read the two `return state` lines. They are not optimisations you add later;
they are the reducer saying "nothing happened", and React's `Object.is` bailout
turns that statement into zero work.

## Worked example — impurity in the action

```tsx
// Wrong: two Strict Mode invocations produce two different ids.
case "noteAdded":
  return [...state, { id: crypto.randomUUID(), text: action.text, at: Date.now() }];

// Right: the handler meets the outside world; the reducer only computes.
dispatch({ type: "noteAdded", id: crypto.randomUUID(), text, at: Date.now() });

case "noteAdded":
  return [...state, { id: action.id, text: action.text, at: action.at }];
```

The second version is also the testable one: `noteReducer(before, action)` is a
pure function you can assert on without fake timers or a mocked `crypto`.

## Worked example — undo

```tsx
type History = { past: string[]; present: string; future: string[] };

case "undone":
  if (state.past.length === 0) return state;          // no-op, same object
  return {
    past: state.past.slice(0, -1),
    present: state.past[state.past.length - 1],
    future: [state.present, ...state.future],
  };

case "edited":
  if (action.text === state.present) return state;
  return { past: [...state.past, state.present], present: action.text, future: [] };
```

`future: []` on an edit is the rule everyone forgets. Without it, redo after an
edit replays a timeline that no longer exists.

## Worked example — delegation without losing the bailout

```tsx
function boardReducer(state: BoardState, action: BoardAction): BoardState {
  if ("cardId" in action) {
    let changed = false;
    const cards = state.cards.map((card) => {
      if (card.id !== action.cardId) return card;
      const next = cardReducer(card, action);
      if (next !== card) changed = true;
      return next;
    });
    return changed ? { ...state, cards } : state;
  }
  …
}
```

`map` always allocates, even when every callback returned its input unchanged.
The `changed` flag is what turns "I mapped over the list" back into "one card
moved": untouched cards keep their identity, so memoized rows stay still, and an
action targeting a card that does not exist returns the board itself.

The other half of the technique is that `cardReducer` is a pure function from a
card and an action to a card. It has no idea boards exist, so its tests need one
object and no React — and when the card rules grow, the board reducer does not.

## Worked example — deriving instead of storing

```tsx
// Storing it: every transition that touches lines must remember to update it,
// and the one that forgets charges the wrong amount.
{ lines: Line[]; totalPence: number }

// Deriving it: there is no total to forget.
const totalPence = state.lines.reduce(
  (sum, line) => sum + line.unitPence * line.quantity,
  0,
);
```

Money in integers, always. `0.1 + 0.2 !== 0.3`, and a rounding error in a
checkout is an incident rather than a bug.

## Common mistakes

- **Command-shaped actions.** `{ type: "setTotal", total }` moves the decision
  to the call site; the reducer becomes a setter with extra typing.
- **No `never` check.** An unhandled action silently returns the current state.
- **Impurity in the reducer.** Strict Mode doubles it; concurrent rendering may
  replay it.
- **Mutating `state`.** React compares references, sees no change, renders
  nothing — and your data really did change, so the bug outlives the fix.
- **Eager initialisation.** `useReducer(r, expensive())` runs on every render.
- **Returning a fresh object for a no-op.** A commit for nothing, and a wave of
  re-renders in a memoized tree.
- **Storing derived values in the reducer.** Totals, error messages, "is valid"
  flags: derive them.
- **One reducer for the whole page.** A reducer is local state with a better
  shape; it is not a store, and a hundred-line switch is a smell.
- **Dispatching in a loop over the same field.** Batching handles it, but it is
  usually a sign the action was too small.

## Interview answer

> **"When would you use `useReducer` instead of `useState`?"**
>
> When the transitions matter more than the values. Three signals: several
> fields always change together; the next state depends on more than one current
> field; or some actions are invalid in some states. Those are exactly the cases
> where scattered setters mean the rules only exist as an emergent property of
> eight handlers.
>
> The concrete wins are that all the rules live in one pure function you can
> unit-test without React; that a discriminated action union plus a `never` check
> in the default branch makes forgetting a case a compile error; and that
> `dispatch` is stable, so a memoized child that takes only `dispatch` never
> re-renders without a single `useCallback`.
>
> The discipline that goes with it: actions are past-tense facts, not commands;
> the reducer must be pure, so ids and timestamps are minted in the handler and
> passed in the payload; derived values are derived, not stored; and an action
> that is invalid right now returns the *same state object*, so React bails out
> instead of committing a no-op.

## Check yourself

1. Three signals that a reducer beats `useState`. Give an example of each.
2. Why past-tense action names? What goes wrong with `setX`-shaped ones?
3. Write the `never` check and explain what it protects you from.
4. Name three impurities that break a reducer and say what React does to expose
   them.
5. What is the difference between `return state` and `return { ...state }` for
   an action that changes nothing?
6. What does the third argument to `useReducer` do, and when is it worth it?
7. Rewrite `{ isLoading, isError, data, error }` as a union. How many
   representable-but-meaningless combinations did you remove?
8. Why must a new edit clear the redo future?
9. Which parts of a form belong in the reducer and which are derived?
10. Why does a memoized child taking only `dispatch` never re-render?
11. How do you delegate to a sub-reducer without losing the parent's bailout?
12. Where does an audit log of transitions belong, given that reducers must be
    pure?
13. Is `useReducer` a replacement for Redux? Say what it does and does not do.
14. Your reducer is a hundred-line switch. What does that suggest?

