## Hint 1

Requirement 2 is the specification. A re-render with no change to `score`
happened; one hook noticed the render and one hook noticed only changes. Work
out which storage — a ref written after commit, or state compared during render
— has which behaviour before writing either.

## Hint 2

The committed version is four lines:

```tsx
const ref = useRef<T | undefined>(undefined);
useEffect(() => {
  ref.current = value;
});
return ref.current;
```

No dependency array, so it is refreshed after every commit. That is exactly why
it moves when nothing changed.

## Hint 3

The rendered version keeps the previous value in state and adjusts during
render:

```tsx
const [previous, setPrevious] = useState<T | undefined>(undefined);
const [current, setCurrent] = useState(value);
if (current !== value) { setPrevious(current); setCurrent(value); }
```

Setting state during render is legal here and React documents it. The decision
left to you is whether the guard is `!==` or `Object.is` — say which you would
use for a value that might be `NaN`.

