## Exercise 6 — `useAsync`

Edit `ex6/solution.tsx`. Export the reducer, the hook and a component.

```ts
export type AsyncState<T> =
  | { status: "idle" }
  | { status: "pending" }
  | { status: "success"; data: T }
  | { status: "failure"; message: string };

export function asyncReducer<T>(state: AsyncState<T>, action: AsyncAction<T>): AsyncState<T>;

export function useAsync<T>(
  run: (signal: AbortSignal) => Promise<T>,
): AsyncState<T> & { reload: () => void };
```

The hook runs `run` on mount and whenever `run`'s identity changes, aborts the
previous call when superseded or unmounted, and never writes state from an
abandoned run. `reload()` re-runs the current job.

`ProfileCard({ userId, fetchProfile })` composes it —
`useAsync(useCallback((signal) => fetchProfile(userId, signal), [userId,
fetchProfile]))` — and renders `Loading…`, `Profile: <name>`, or
`Failed: <message>` in a `role="status"` paragraph, plus a `Reload` button.

The gate calls `asyncReducer` directly with values to check the transitions,
then renders and changes `userId` mid-flight, requiring the first request's
signal to be aborted and its late resolution to change nothing.

```
uv run dojo test react v3ra06_composed_hooks ex6
```

